  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Another damp and cool night ahead. Turning drier \nwhile remaining breezy Thursday and Friday.  \n\nImproving weather conditions tonight into Thursday as drier air \nfilters in from the northeast. Some breaks were already visible\non satellite from northern VA points north and east as the \ndrier air erodes the wedge. Strong 1040 mb high pressure over \nsoutheast Quebec will continue to wedge south through Thursday \nallowing for decreasing rain chances and cloud cover. At the \nsame time, low pressure will begin to strengthen off the \nDelmarva/NC coast before riding north toward southern New \nEngland as a pretty impressive Nor'easter takes shape Friday \ninto Saturday. \n\nFor the remainder of the evening, expect pockets of drizzle and \nconfined to locations west of the Blue Ridge and down into central \nVA. Elsewhere conditions will remain mostly dry with cloudy/mostly \ncloudy skies expected. Overnight lows tonight will fall into \nthe upper 40s and mid 50s (mid 40s mountains). Winds will remain\nelevated out of the northeast, with gusts to around 15-30 mph \npossible at times to the east of the Blue Ridge.\n\nDrier weather returns Thursday and Friday although breezy conditions \nwill remain as the upper level trough axis passes overhead. This \nwill lead to more clouds than sunshine Thursday with better clearing \nFriday as more subsidence works into the region. Coastal low \npressure will continue to deepen off the Delmarva coast leading to \ngusty north to northeast winds for both Thursday and Friday \nafternoon. Gale conditions will be likely across the open and middle \nwaters as well as out along the Atlantic coast given the tightened \npressure gradient. Gusts of 20-30 mph are expected with the highest \nlikely along the waters and toward the ridges. Temperatures \nwill stay below normal as a result, with highs in the 60s \nThursday and upper 60s to near 70 on Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Breezy and warmer this weekend, but forecast remains \nuncertain as Nor'easter impacts Northeast U.S. \n\nAnomalously strong surface ridge (by late September standards) \nfinally weakens this weekend as a strong Nor'easter develops off the \ncoast of Southern New England/LI. Ensemble members still show quite \na bit of spread regarding the exact track of the Nor'easter. A few \nmembers show the possibility the low tracks closer to the NJ / \nDelmarva coast this weekend. Regardless of exact position, we are \nlikely to be on the drier (subsidence) side of the system with \nnorthwest winds bringing in breezy, warmer, and mostly dry \nconditions. If the Nor'easter tracks closer to our area that could \nbring some rain chances to the area, especially along/east of I-95, \nalong with the potential for increasing winds. Safe to say that \nthere isn't a definite trend for this weekend, so be sure to keep \ntrack of the forecast for any changes in the coming days.\n\nThe Nor'easter is eventually pushed offshore at the start of next \nweek as high pressure builds in from the OH Valley and Great Lakes. \nMid-level ridging builds over much of the eastern CONUS during the \nmiddle of next week, marking the return of much warmer temperatures \nand continued dry conditions.",
